---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy: Aurora Go/No-Go"
  description="What this site stores, where your browser connects, and how the optional location check handles your coordinates. No accounts, no cookies, no ads."
  path="/privacy/"
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lede">Short version: no accounts, no cookies, no ads, and your location is only used if you ask.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Your location</h2>
    <p>
      The site never asks for your location on its own. On the <a href="/my-location/">My location</a> page you can
      press a button to share it, or type coordinates. Either way:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the coordinates are rounded to two decimal places, which is about 1 km;</li>
      <li>they are saved in your browser's local storage, so the page remembers your spot, and nowhere else by this site;</li>
      <li>"Forget my spot" removes them, as does clearing your browser data.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where your browser connects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>This site, hosted by Cloudflare.</strong> Like any web host, Cloudflare processes technical request
        data such as IP address and browser type to deliver pages and stop abuse. See
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>NOAA.</strong> Space weather readings are fetched by your browser directly from
        <code>services.swpc.noaa.gov</code>, a United States government server. NOAA sees your IP address, as any site
        you visit does. No location or personal detail is sent with those requests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloud forecasts.</strong> Your browser asks this site for the forecast, sending only the rounded
        coordinates of the place being checked. This site then asks MET Norway. MET Norway sees the coordinates and
        this site's server, not you. Results are cached for up to six hours against the rounded coordinates so the
        same forecast is not requested over and over. This site keeps no log linking those coordinates to you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Visit counting.</strong> Cloudflare adds its Web Analytics script to these pages. It counts visits and
        page views and measures how quickly pages load, which tells us which pages are being used. According to
        Cloudflare, the script sets no cookies, stores nothing in your browser and does not fingerprint visitors. The
        script file comes from <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code> and it reports to this site's own address.
        See <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare's description of Web Analytics</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your saved spot, if you set one (local storage).</li>
      <li>A copy of recent cloud forecasts for about 20 minutes, to avoid asking again (session storage).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>No cookies are set by this site.</p>

    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No accounts, logins or sign-up forms.</li>
      <li>No advertising scripts at the moment.</li>
      <li>No selling or sharing of data. There is none to sell.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you email us</h2>
    <p>We receive your email address and whatever you write. It is used only to deal with your message.</p>

    <h2>If this changes</h2>
    <p>
      If advertising or other analytics are added later, this page will be updated first to say what is used, what it
      collects and how to opt out where that applies.
    </p>

    <p class="small muted">Last updated {site.updated}.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
